# Percentages and Ratios: Comparing Quantities

> Percentages and ratios both compare quantities — percentages against a
> base of 100, ratios part-to-part. Most "real world" maths is one of
> these two in disguise.

## 1. Percent Means Per Hundred

@math label="eq:percent-def"
x\% = \frac{x}{100}
@/math

- 45% of ₹800: $\frac{45}{100} \times 800 = 360$
- What percent is 18 of 60? $\frac{18}{60} \times 100 = 30\%$

## 2. Percentage Change

[definition]
Percentage change = $\frac{\text{new} - \text{old}}{\text{old}} \times
100$. The denominator is always the ORIGINAL value — the most common
exam mistake is dividing by the new one.
[/definition]

## Example

Price rises from ₹250 to ₹300:
$\frac{50}{250} \times 100 = +20\%$.
Falling back from ₹300 to ₹250: $\frac{-50}{300} \times 100 = -16.7\%$.
Equal absolute steps are NOT equal percentage steps.

## 3. Successive Changes Multiply

A +10% rise followed by −10% fall does not return to the start:

@math label="eq:successive"
1.10 \times 0.90 = 0.99 \quad (\text{a net } 1\% \text{ loss})
@/math

## Example

₹500 → +10% → ₹550 → −10% → ₹495. Each change applies to whatever the
value has become, not to the original.

## 4. Ratios: Part-to-Part Comparison

Divide ₹600 between Anu and Bilal in ratio $2 : 3$: that's $5$ parts,
so one part = ₹120 — Anu ₹240, Bilal ₹360.

| Ratio | As fractions of total |
| --- | --- |
| 2 : 3 | 2/5 and 3/5 |
| 1 : 1 : 2 | 1/4, 1/4, 1/2 |

Proportion check: $\frac{240}{360} = \frac{2}{3}$ ✓

## 5. A Share Picture

The ₹600 split above as a picture:

@chart pie title="₹600 split in ratio 2 : 3" labels:
Anu (2 parts)
Bilal (3 parts)

data:
240
360
@/chart

[important title="Ratios scale, they don't add"]
Doubling BOTH terms keeps the ratio identical: $2{:}3 = 4{:}6 = 20{:}30$.
Adding the same number to both terms changes it instead — $3{:}4 \neq 2{:}3$.
[/important]

## Key Takeaways

- Percent change divides by the original; successive changes multiply.
- Ratio splits: total ÷ (sum of parts) × parts.
- Equal steps up and down in percent never cancel exactly.
